regcf-wizard.l4:770:1-35:
  RaiseAssessment OF TRUE, 5000000, EMPTY, `financial statements reviewed by an independent public accountant`, "Your raise aggregates to $500,000, above the tier-1 ceiling of $124,000. A review by an independent public accountant is required up to $618,000; a first-time issuer may stay on a review up to $1,235,000 (17 CFR 227.201(t)(2)-(3)).", (LIST "Run the offering through exactly one intermediary — a registered broker or funding portal — that complies with section 4A(a) of the Securities Act, and conduct it exclusively on that intermediary's platform. Using more than one intermediary costs you the exemption (17 CFR 227.100(a)(3) and its Instruction).", "Have your offering information publicly available on the intermediary's platform for at least 21 days before any securities are sold (17 CFR 227.303(a)(2)).", "File a Form C offering statement with the Commission, and provide it to investors and to the intermediary, before the offering begins. It must carry everything section 227.201 requires (17 CFR 227.203(a)(1)).", "The intermediary must deliver educational materials when it opens an investor's account, and must have a reasonable basis for believing each investor is within their own investment limits (17 CFR 227.302(b)(1), 227.303(b)(1)). Those are the intermediary's to satisfy, and this check does not ask about them.", "Each investor must be within their own 12-month Regulation Crowdfunding limit (17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)). Use the investment-limit check for that."), (LIST "File a Form C-AR annual report within 120 days of the end of each fiscal year, and keep filing until one of the five conditions in Rule 202(b) occurs (17 CFR 227.202(a), 227.203(b)(1)).", "Once one of those conditions occurs, file a Form C-TR within 5 business days to bring the reporting duty to an end (17 CFR 227.203(b)(3)).", "File a Form C-U progress update within 5 business days of reaching the relevant point in the offering (17 CFR 227.203(a)(3)(i)).", "Miss the annual reports and you are disqualified from your NEXT Regulation Crowdfunding offering for two years (17 CFR 227.100(b)(5)) — though the default does not cost you the exemption for an offering already made (17 CFR 227.100(a)(4))."), (LIST "This check answers two of the four conditions in Rule 100(a): that your company is eligible, and that the raise is within the 12-month offering limit. The other two — the single complying intermediary, and each investor's own limit — are under 'what you must still line up'.", "The bad-actor disqualification is a single yes/no question here. Rule 503(a) is a regime in its own right: which people are covered, how far the look-back runs, and a reasonable-care exception. Take advice before answering it.", "The offering limit aggregates only what was sold in reliance on section 4(a)(6). Sales under other exemptions are not added in. Whether a concurrent offering is integrated with this one is a separate question, which Rule 100(e) refers out to 17 CFR 230.152.", "Rules 201(t)(1) and (t)(2) say that if higher-assurance financial statements are already available, the issuer must provide those instead. That trigger turns on what your company happens to hold, and is not modelled here."), "17 CFR Part 227 as in force on 2026-07-23. The dollar figures are adjusted for inflation on roughly a five-yearly cycle; the last adjustment took effect 2022-09-20 and the next is due around 2027."
regcf-wizard.l4:771:1-40:
  RaiseAssessment OF FALSE, 5000000, (LIST "Your company has sold under Regulation Crowdfunding before and has not filed the annual reports it owed in the two years before this offering statement, so it may not raise again until it has caught up (17 CFR 227.100(b)(5))."), `financial statements reviewed by an independent public accountant`, "Your raise aggregates to $500,000, above the tier-1 ceiling of $124,000. A review by an independent public accountant is required up to $618,000; a first-time issuer may stay on a review up to $1,235,000 (17 CFR 227.201(t)(2)-(3)).", (LIST "Run the offering through exactly one intermediary — a registered broker or funding portal — that complies with section 4A(a) of the Securities Act, and conduct it exclusively on that intermediary's platform. Using more than one intermediary costs you the exemption (17 CFR 227.100(a)(3) and its Instruction).", "Have your offering information publicly available on the intermediary's platform for at least 21 days before any securities are sold (17 CFR 227.303(a)(2)).", "File a Form C offering statement with the Commission, and provide it to investors and to the intermediary, before the offering begins. It must carry everything section 227.201 requires (17 CFR 227.203(a)(1)).", "The intermediary must deliver educational materials when it opens an investor's account, and must have a reasonable basis for believing each investor is within their own investment limits (17 CFR 227.302(b)(1), 227.303(b)(1)). Those are the intermediary's to satisfy, and this check does not ask about them.", "Each investor must be within their own 12-month Regulation Crowdfunding limit (17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)). Use the investment-limit check for that."), (LIST "File a Form C-AR annual report within 120 days of the end of each fiscal year, and keep filing until one of the five conditions in Rule 202(b) occurs (17 CFR 227.202(a), 227.203(b)(1)).", "Once one of those conditions occurs, file a Form C-TR within 5 business days to bring the reporting duty to an end (17 CFR 227.203(b)(3)).", "File a Form C-U progress update within 5 business days of reaching the relevant point in the offering (17 CFR 227.203(a)(3)(i)).", "Miss the annual reports and you are disqualified from your NEXT Regulation Crowdfunding offering for two years (17 CFR 227.100(b)(5)) — though the default does not cost you the exemption for an offering already made (17 CFR 227.100(a)(4))."), (LIST "This check answers two of the four conditions in Rule 100(a): that your company is eligible, and that the raise is within the 12-month offering limit. The other two — the single complying intermediary, and each investor's own limit — are under 'what you must still line up'.", "The bad-actor disqualification is a single yes/no question here. Rule 503(a) is a regime in its own right: which people are covered, how far the look-back runs, and a reasonable-care exception. Take advice before answering it.", "The offering limit aggregates only what was sold in reliance on section 4(a)(6). Sales under other exemptions are not added in. Whether a concurrent offering is integrated with this one is a separate question, which Rule 100(e) refers out to 17 CFR 230.152.", "Rules 201(t)(1) and (t)(2) say that if higher-assurance financial statements are already available, the issuer must provide those instead. That trigger turns on what your company happens to hold, and is not modelled here."), "17 CFR Part 227 as in force on 2026-07-23. The dollar figures are adjusted for inflation on roughly a five-yearly cycle; the last adjustment took effect 2022-09-20 and the next is due around 2027."
regcf-wizard.l4:772:1-41:
  RaiseAssessment OF FALSE, 1000000, (LIST "What you want to raise, added to what you have already sold under Regulation Crowdfunding in the last 12 months, comes to $5,500,000, which is more than the $5,000,000 an issuer may sell in reliance on section 4(a)(6) in any 12-month period (17 CFR 227.100(a)(1))."), `financial statements audited by an independent public accountant`, "Your raise aggregates to $5,500,000, past the point at which a review will do: above $618,000 a repeat issuer must be audited, and even a first-time issuer must be audited above $1,235,000 (17 CFR 227.201(t)(3)).", (LIST "Run the offering through exactly one intermediary — a registered broker or funding portal — that complies with section 4A(a) of the Securities Act, and conduct it exclusively on that intermediary's platform. Using more than one intermediary costs you the exemption (17 CFR 227.100(a)(3) and its Instruction).", "Have your offering information publicly available on the intermediary's platform for at least 21 days before any securities are sold (17 CFR 227.303(a)(2)).", "File a Form C offering statement with the Commission, and provide it to investors and to the intermediary, before the offering begins. It must carry everything section 227.201 requires (17 CFR 227.203(a)(1)).", "The intermediary must deliver educational materials when it opens an investor's account, and must have a reasonable basis for believing each investor is within their own investment limits (17 CFR 227.302(b)(1), 227.303(b)(1)). Those are the intermediary's to satisfy, and this check does not ask about them.", "Each investor must be within their own 12-month Regulation Crowdfunding limit (17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)). Use the investment-limit check for that."), (LIST "File a Form C-AR annual report within 120 days of the end of each fiscal year, and keep filing until one of the five conditions in Rule 202(b) occurs (17 CFR 227.202(a), 227.203(b)(1)).", "Once one of those conditions occurs, file a Form C-TR within 5 business days to bring the reporting duty to an end (17 CFR 227.203(b)(3)).", "File a Form C-U progress update within 5 business days of reaching the relevant point in the offering (17 CFR 227.203(a)(3)(i)).", "Miss the annual reports and you are disqualified from your NEXT Regulation Crowdfunding offering for two years (17 CFR 227.100(b)(5)) — though the default does not cost you the exemption for an offering already made (17 CFR 227.100(a)(4))."), (LIST "This check answers two of the four conditions in Rule 100(a): that your company is eligible, and that the raise is within the 12-month offering limit. The other two — the single complying intermediary, and each investor's own limit — are under 'what you must still line up'.", "The bad-actor disqualification is a single yes/no question here. Rule 503(a) is a regime in its own right: which people are covered, how far the look-back runs, and a reasonable-care exception. Take advice before answering it.", "The offering limit aggregates only what was sold in reliance on section 4(a)(6). Sales under other exemptions are not added in. Whether a concurrent offering is integrated with this one is a separate question, which Rule 100(e) refers out to 17 CFR 230.152.", "Rules 201(t)(1) and (t)(2) say that if higher-assurance financial statements are already available, the issuer must provide those instead. That trigger turns on what your company happens to hold, and is not modelled here."), "17 CFR Part 227 as in force on 2026-07-23. The dollar figures are adjusted for inflation on roughly a five-yearly cycle; the last adjustment took effect 2022-09-20 and the next is due around 2027."
regcf-wizard.l4:773:1-57:
  InvestmentLimitAnswer OF FALSE, (JUST OF 10000), 0, (JUST OF 10000), 200000, "17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)(i)", "Rule 100(a)(2)(i) governs, because at least one of your annual income ($60,000) and your net worth ($200,000) is below $124,000. Your limit is 5% of the greater of the two ($200,000), or $2,500, whichever is the larger — which comes to $10,000.", (LIST "The limit is measured against the GREATER of your annual income and your net worth. Until 2021-03-15 it was measured against the LESSER, and Release 33-10884 substituted the word. A guide that still says 'lesser' is not carrying a stale number; it is carrying a policy the Commission abandoned, and it will understate what you may lawfully invest whenever your income and your net worth differ.", "The limit runs across ALL Regulation Crowdfunding offerings you invest in during the 12 months before the transaction, not just this one.", "Rule 100(a)(2) does not define 'annual income' or 'net worth'. Instruction 1 sends you to the accredited-investor calculation in Rule 501, and Instruction 2 allows spouses to calculate jointly subject to a per-individual cap. Neither is modelled here: the figures are read as your own.", "'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value.")
regcf-wizard.l4:774:1-52:
  InvestmentLimitAnswer OF FALSE, (JUST OF 124000), 0, (JUST OF 124000), 3000000, "17 CFR 227.100(a)(2)(ii)", "Rule 100(a)(2)(ii) governs, because both your annual income ($3,000,000) and your net worth ($3,000,000) are at least $124,000. Your limit is 10% of the greater of the two ($3,000,000), capped at $124,000 — which comes to $124,000.", (LIST "The limit is measured against the GREATER of your annual income and your net worth. Until 2021-03-15 it was measured against the LESSER, and Release 33-10884 substituted the word. A guide that still says 'lesser' is not carrying a stale number; it is carrying a policy the Commission abandoned, and it will understate what you may lawfully invest whenever your income and your net worth differ.", "The limit runs across ALL Regulation Crowdfunding offerings you invest in during the 12 months before the transaction, not just this one.", "Rule 100(a)(2) does not define 'annual income' or 'net worth'. Instruction 1 sends you to the accredited-investor calculation in Rule 501, and Instruction 2 allows spouses to calculate jointly subject to a per-individual cap. Neither is modelled here: the figures are read as your own.", "'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value.")
regcf-wizard.l4:775:1-57:
  InvestmentLimitAnswer OF TRUE, NOTHING, 9000, NOTHING, 200000, "None. Rule 100(a)(2) bites only where the purchaser is not an accredited investor — a carve-out added on 2021-03-15.", "You are an accredited investor, so Regulation Crowdfunding sets no limit on what you may invest (17 CFR 227.100(a)(2), as amended with effect from 2021-03-15). The figure shown is what your limit would be if you were not: $10,000.", (LIST "The limit is measured against the GREATER of your annual income and your net worth. Until 2021-03-15 it was measured against the LESSER, and Release 33-10884 substituted the word. A guide that still says 'lesser' is not carrying a stale number; it is carrying a policy the Commission abandoned, and it will understate what you may lawfully invest whenever your income and your net worth differ.", "The limit runs across ALL Regulation Crowdfunding offerings you invest in during the 12 months before the transaction, not just this one.", "Rule 100(a)(2) does not define 'annual income' or 'net worth'. Instruction 1 sends you to the accredited-investor calculation in Rule 501, and Instruction 2 allows spouses to calculate jointly subject to a per-individual cap. Neither is modelled here: the figures are read as your own.", "'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value.")
regcf-wizard.l4:776:1-44:
  ResaleAnswer OF FALSE, 265, "The securities were issued 100 days ago. Rule 501(a) restricts transfer for 365 days from issuance, and none of the four exceptions in Rule 501(a)(1)-(4) applies.", (LIST "The one-year period is measured here as 365 days from issuance, and the last of those days is read as OUTSIDE the period. Rule 501(a) says only 'the one-year period beginning when the securities were issued' and does not say whether the first anniversary falls inside it. That ambiguity is in the rule, not in this model, and no encoding can settle it.", "A leap year inside the period would make the true anniversary a day later than the count used here.", "'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value.", "Rule 501 restricts transfer by a purchaser in the crowdfunding transaction. It does not, by itself, tell you what a subsequent holder may do.")
regcf-wizard.l4:777:1-61:
  ResaleAnswer OF TRUE, 0, "The transferee is an accredited investor, which Rule 501(a)(2) excepts from the restriction.", (LIST "The one-year period is measured here as 365 days from issuance, and the last of those days is read as OUTSIDE the period. Rule 501(a) says only 'the one-year period beginning when the securities were issued' and does not say whether the first anniversary falls inside it. That ambiguity is in the rule, not in this model, and no encoding can settle it.", "A leap year inside the period would make the true anniversary a day later than the count used here.", "'Accredited investor' takes its meaning from Rule 501 of Regulation D. This check takes your answer at face value.", "Rule 501 restricts transfer by a purchaser in the crowdfunding transaction. It does not, by itself, tell you what a subsequent holder may do.")
regcf-wizard.l4:778:1-52:
  ReportingExitAnswer OF TRUE, (LIST "Your company has filed at least one annual report since its most recent sale under this Part, and has fewer than 300 holders of record (17 CFR 227.202(b)(2))."), (LIST "File a Form C-AR annual report within 120 days of the end of each fiscal year, and post it on your website (17 CFR 227.202(a), 227.203(b)(1)).", "When one of the five conditions occurs, file a Form C-TR within 5 business days. Until you do, the duty stands (17 CFR 227.203(b)(3))."), (LIST "The Form C-TR deadline is 5 BUSINESS days, and the annual report deadline is 120 plain days. The corpus counts both in plain days, because the deadline arithmetic has no business-day calendar. Where a weekend or a holiday falls inside the window, the true business-day deadline is later than the count used here.", "This check answers whether the duty MAY come to an end. It does not end by itself: filing the Form C-TR is what ends it.", "Failing to file does not cost you the exemption for an offering already made (17 CFR 227.100(a)(4)), but it does disqualify you from your next Regulation Crowdfunding offering for two years (17 CFR 227.100(b)(5)).")
regcf-wizard.l4:779:1-59:
  ReportingExitAnswer OF FALSE, EMPTY, (LIST "File a Form C-AR annual report within 120 days of the end of each fiscal year, and post it on your website (17 CFR 227.202(a), 227.203(b)(1)).", "When one of the five conditions occurs, file a Form C-TR within 5 business days. Until you do, the duty stands (17 CFR 227.203(b)(3))."), (LIST "The Form C-TR deadline is 5 BUSINESS days, and the annual report deadline is 120 plain days. The corpus counts both in plain days, because the deadline arithmetic has no business-day calendar. Where a weekend or a holiday falls inside the window, the true business-day deadline is later than the count used here.", "This check answers whether the duty MAY come to an end. It does not end by itself: filing the Form C-TR is what ends it.", "Failing to file does not cost you the exemption for an offering already made (17 CFR 227.100(a)(4)), but it does disqualify you from your next Regulation Crowdfunding offering for two years (17 CFR 227.100(b)(5)).")
